|---|---|---|
| **Novelty** | 6/10 | Reformulating meta-learning as probabilistic inference is conceptually distinctive; module/grammar decomposition is well-motivated and implemented with Gumbel-softmax particle filtering |
| **Technical soundness** | 6/10 | Clean probabilistic formulation, correctly derived particle filter; training/test-time mismatch and particle count sensitivity unanalyzed |
| **Empirical support** | 4/10 | Only low-dimensional synthetic tasks with known structure; no standard benchmarks; sparse-feedback claims supported only qualitatively (single episode figures); no transformer/in-context learning comparison |
| **Significance** | 5/10 | Interesting proof-of-concept; practical utility undemonstrated beyond synthetic domains; no evaluation against modern in-context learners |
| **Clarity** | 7/10 | Clean math, strong ablations (Figure 3), full code/data release — notable for open-science |

### Calibration Comparison

| Paper | Avg Score | Outcome | Comparison to this paper |
|---|---|---|---|
| **COM** | 0.0 | Reject | Far below — missing results entirely, incoherent prose |
| **DualRes** | 2.5 | Reject | Below — critical theoretical gap, unreadable tables |
| **SURE** | 4.4 | Reject | Slightly below — missing key baseline, numerical errors; this paper has cleaner ablations but narrower scope |
| **MetaOCDN** | 5.0 | Reject | Comparable — both have sound core idea, limited ablations of key components, evaluation scope issues |
